AudioBookBinder — macOS modernization
====================================

Requires macOS 13 or later. Build with Xcode 26 or later (macOS 26 SDK).
Liquid Glass uses the native AppKit toolbar and segmented control appearance
on macOS 26+. Older supported systems use their native control appearance.
The app follows the system's appearance and accessibility settings.

Build and test
--------------

    git submodule update --init --recursive
    ./scripts/test.sh

If Xcode lives elsewhere, set DEVELOPER_DIR to its Contents/Developer folder.
The script builds universal arm64/x86_64 GUI and CLI executables, runs real AAC
encoding/decoding tests, checks Unicode metadata, artwork and QuickTime
chapters, and tests cancellation, input protection, failed conversions, and
hour-based volume splitting. Use CONFIGURATION=Debug for a debug build.
Optional ffprobe checks independently inspect chapter timing and metadata.
No testing frameworks or third-party Python packages are required.

Build products:
    build/DerivedData/Build/Products/Release/AudioBookBinder.app
    build/DerivedData/Build/Products/Release/abbinder

The test script disables signing so CI needs no developer account. For a
sandboxed local app, build in Xcode using its ad-hoc signing setting, or sign
the generated app locally:

    codesign --force --sign - --options runtime \
      --entitlements AudioBookBinder.entitlements \
      build/DerivedData/Build/Products/Release/AudioBookBinder.app

Distribution still requires your own Developer ID signing and notarization.

Using the app
-------------

Add audio files or drag files/folders into the list. Edit the book details in
the sidebar, arrange chapters using the Chapters menu, and choose artwork
with Choose Cover or the Cover art tab. Expand a chapter and select its audio
file to preview it. Create Audiobook opens the native Save panel. If the book
needs multiple volumes, choose an export folder to authorize writing all of
them. Audio quality and volume duration are available in Settings.

To edit an existing .m4b, choose File > Edit Audiobook Metadata… (Shift-Command-O).
The editor loads its title, author, narrator, album, genre, and embedded cover.
Edit or clear those fields, choose a PNG/JPEG cover or remove the current one,
then click Save (Command-S). In the Chapters tab, edit chapter titles and start
times, add a marker by splitting the selected chapter, or remove selected
markers. Times accept HH:MM:SS.mmm, MM:SS.mmm, or seconds; the first marker is
always at zero and the last chapter runs to the end of the audio. Removing a
marker merges its interval into the preceding chapter; removing the first
marker extends the next chapter back to zero. Removing all markers clears the
book’s chapters. Chapters are saved in their existing QuickTime/Nero formats;
books without markers get QuickTime chapters when chapters are added.

Saving replaces the selected file without re-encoding audio or changing volume
numbers or unrelated tags. Chapters remain intact unless edited in their tab.
Closing or quitting prompts for unsaved changes; Don’t Save leaves the file intact.
Edits are staged in a temporary copy, so a failed metadata write leaves the
original intact. If another app changes the file while it is open, reopen it
before saving. Large books need enough free space for a temporary copy.

Conversion stages all volumes before replacing any outputs. A failed or
cancelled conversion preserves existing destination files. Inputs cannot be
used as outputs, including symlink and hard-link aliases. Metadata/chapter
errors are reported instead of announcing success.

Validation on October 25, 2026: Xcode 27.1 / macOS SDK 27.0, Apple silicon,
macOS 27.0.1. Both architectures compile; older macOS versions and Intel
hardware require separate runtime testing. See TESTING.md for recorded checks.

= Command line utility: abbinder 
As of 1.18 it's part of main bundler. Path to the utility is:
    build/DerivedData/Build/Products/Release/abbinder

== Usage
abbinder [-hsv] [-c 1|2] [-r samplerate] [-a author] [-t title] 
  [-i filelist] -o outfile [@chapter_1@ infile @chapter_2@ ...]
        -a author       set book author
        -c 1|2          number of channels in audiobook. Default: 2
        -C file.png     cover image
        -e              alias for -E ''
        -E template     make each file a chapter with name defined by template
                            %N - chapter number
                            %a - artis (obtained from source file)
                            %t - title (obtained from source file)
        -h              show this message
        -i file         get input files list from file, "-" for standard input
        -q              quiet mode (no output)
        -r rate         sample rate of audiobook. Default: 44100
        -s              skip errors and go on with conversion
        -t title        set book title
        -v              print some info on files being converted

Attributes for -a and -t arguments (author and title) should be in UTF8.
Only jpeg and png are supported as cover page format. 
Valid sample rate values:
    8000, 11025, 12000, 16000, 22050, 24000, 32000, 44100, 48000

Chapters could be set either by chapter markers in file list or by
treating each source file as a chapter.  In former case chapter name is
provided by user and should be enclosed in '@' characters to distinguish
it from file names. e.g.:
    abbinder -o book.m4b '@Chapter 1@' track01.mp3 '@Chapter 2@' track02.mp3

Chapter names in the latter case are generated for each input file and 
may contain some escaped sequences that would be expanded when creating 
a chapter. Template is provided as argument for -E switch. 
Available sequences:
    %N - number of chapter, starts from 1
    %a - value of 'artist' tag from source file
    %t - value of 'title' tag from source file

So previous example could be rewritten like:
    abbinder -o book.m4b -E 'Chapter %N' track01.mp3 track02.mp3

== Examples

Convert one file to m4b
  bash$ abbinder -o book.m4b myfile.mp3

Convert several files to mono m4b with sample rate 8000
  bash$ abbinder -r 8000 -c 1 -o book.m4b myfile.mp3 myfile2.mp3

Convert all mp3 files in directory and set author, title and cover image
  bash$ find ./dir -name '*mp3' > list
  bash$ abbinder -i list -a Author -t Title -C ~/cover.png -o out.m4b

Convert three-chapter book with explicit chapter names:
  bash$ abbinder -o book.m4b '@The very beginning@' 01.mp3 \
      '@In the middle of something@' 02.mp3 \
      '@Ashes to ashes and dust to dust@' 03.mp3

Convert several short-stories files and make each of them a chapter
  bash$ abbinder -o stories.m4b -E '%t' story01.mp3 story02.mp3 story03.mp3
